ref_dict.checks:
\begin{proposition}\label{checks}
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be smooth projective complex algebraic varieties. Then
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If $Z\subset X$ is a smooth projective subvariety of codimension $c$, $\pi:\text{Bl}_Z(X)\to X$ is the blow-up of $X$ along $Z$, and $E$ is the exceptional divisor of the blowup, then $$H_{\ZZ}(X)-H_{\ZZ}(Z) = H_{\ZZ}(\text{Bl}_Z(X)) - H_{\ZZ}(E).$$
        \item $H_{\ZZ}(X\times Y) = H_{\ZZ}(X)H_{\ZZ}(Y)$ 
    \end{enumerate}
    In particular, $H_{\ZZ}$ extends to a function
    \[
    H_{\text{vir},\ZZ}\colon K_0(\text{Var}_\CC)\to R.
    \]
\end{proposition}

expanded_sketch:
To prove the main theorem, the authors “introduc[e] an \emph{integral virtual Hodge function} $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}$,” which is “well-defined on the Grothendieck ring of varieties $K_0(\text{Var}_\mathbb{C})$” and has the key property that for smooth projective $X$ “the integral Hodge structure, and in particular the integral cohomology groups, of $X$ can be read off from $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}(X)$.” The “main technical point” is to define the target ring so that $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}$ is multiplicative, i.e. $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}(X\times Y)=H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}(X)H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}(Y)$. They extend $H_\mathbb{Z}$ to the virtual integral Hodge function $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}$ using the following proposition.

\begin{proposition}\label{checks}
    Let $X$ and $Y$ be smooth projective complex algebraic varieties. Then
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item If $Z\subset X$ is a smooth projective subvariety of codimension $c$, $\pi:\text{Bl}_Z(X)\to X$ is the blow-up of $X$ along $Z$, and $E$ is the exceptional divisor of the blowup, then $$H_{\ZZ}(X)-H_{\ZZ}(Z) = H_{\ZZ}(\text{Bl}_Z(X)) - H_{\ZZ}(E).$$
        \item $H_{\ZZ}(X\times Y) = H_{\ZZ}(X)H_{\ZZ}(Y)$ 
    \end{enumerate}
    In particular, $H_{\ZZ}$ extends to a function
    \[
    H_{\text{vir},\ZZ}\colon K_0(\text{Var}_\CC)\to R.
    \]
\end{proposition}

More precisely, this proposition (which takes values on complex varieties which are not necessarily smooth or projective) shows that it extends “to a function $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}$ on the Grothendieck ring of varieties.” Finally, they “use the existence of $H_{\text{vir},\mathbb{Z}}$” later to establish the main theorem, i.e. apply this invariant in the $K$-equivalence situation to conclude $H^i(X,\mathbb{Z})\simeq H^i(X',\mathbb{Z})$ for all $i.

