expanded_sketch:
They “reduce” the main theorem to the hypersurface case via Katz’s theorem:
\begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:katz}
Let \(X_{0} \subset \mathbf{P}^{n}\) be a smooth hypersurface.  Then
the Riemann Hypothesis holds for \(X_{0}\).
\end{theorem}
“In this paper, we will use a degeneration argument to reduce the general case of the Riemann Hypothesis for smooth proper varieties to the hypersurface case covered by \begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:katz}
Let \(X_{0} \subset \mathbf{P}^{n}\) be a smooth hypersurface.  Then
the Riemann Hypothesis holds for \(X_{0}\).
\end{theorem},” with the “engine” being “Artin’s vanishing theorem … and an elementary ‘perverse degeneration lemma’,” together with “some properties of perverse sheaves.”

They actually prove the more general weak form:
\begin{theorem}\label{theorem:wrh}
Let \(X_{0}\) be a separated scheme of finite type over
\(\mathbf{F}_{q}\). Then for every integer \(i\), all eigenvalues of Frobenius acting on
\(\mathrm{H}^{i}_{c}(X;\overline{\mathbf{Q}}_{\ell})\) have
\(q\)-weight \(\leq i\).
\end{theorem}
and then deduce the main theorem as follows: by \begin{theorem}\label{theorem:wrh}
Let \(X_{0}\) be a separated scheme of finite type over
\(\mathbf{F}_{q}\). Then for every integer \(i\), all eigenvalues of Frobenius acting on
\(\mathrm{H}^{i}_{c}(X;\overline{\mathbf{Q}}_{\ell})\) have
\(q\)-weight \(\leq i\).
\end{theorem}, any Frobenius eigenvalue \(\alpha\) on \(\mathrm{H}^i(X;\overline{\mathbf{Q}}_{\ell})\) satisfies \(|\iota(\alpha)| \le q^{i/2}\). Using Poincaré duality
\[\mathrm{H}^i(X; \overline{\mathbf{Q}}_\ell) \simeq \mathrm{H}^{2d-i}(X; \overline{\mathbf{Q}}_\ell)^{\vee} \otimes \overline{\mathbf{Q}}_\ell(-d),\]
\(q^{d}/\alpha\) is also an eigenvalue on \(\mathrm{H}^{2d-i}\), hence \(\left|\iota(q^{d}/\alpha)\right|\le q^{d-i/2}\), equivalently \(|\iota(\alpha)|\ge q^{i/2}\). Combining both inequalities gives \(|\iota(\alpha)|=q^{i/2}\), i.e. weight exactly \(i\), completing the proof of the main theorem.

For \begin{theorem}
\label{theorem:katz}
Let \(X_{0} \subset \mathbf{P}^{n}\) be a smooth hypersurface.  Then
the Riemann Hypothesis holds for \(X_{0}\).
\end{theorem}, Katz’s proof is described as “in effect a specialization argument”: “to establish the Riemann Hypothesis for \emph{all} smooth hypersurfaces of degree \(d\) in \(\mathbf{P}^n\), it is enough to find just \emph{one} smooth hypersurface of degree \(d\) in \(\mathbf{P}^n\) for which the Riemann Hypothesis holds,” and such examples are given by “diagonal hypersurfaces” when \((d,\mathrm{char}\,\mathbf{F}_q)=1\) (Weil, via “basic properties of Gauss sums”) and by the “Gabber hypersurface” when \(d\) is divisible by the characteristic (Katz, again using “properties of Gauss sums”).

theorem.content:
\label{MainTh2}
    Let $G$ be a countable discrete group with property $\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{PHP}}$  and let $\omega\in Z^2(G,\mathbb T)$ be a 2-cocycle. Then the  reduced twisted group \Cs-algebra $\mathrm{C}^\ast_{\rm r}(G,\omega)$ is completely selfless. 

    In particular, $\mathrm{C}^\ast_{\rm r}(G,\omega)$ is simple, pure, has stable rank one, and a unique trace that is also the unique quasitracial state.

ref_dict.MainTh2:
\begin{introtheorem}\label{MainTh2}
    Let $G$ be a countable discrete group with property $\mathrm{P}_{\mathrm{PHP}}$  and let $\omega\in Z^2(G,\mathbb T)$ be a 2-cocycle. Then the  reduced twisted group \Cs-algebra $\Csr(G,\omega)$ is completely selfless. 

    In particular, $\Csr(G,\omega)$ is simple, pure, has stable rank one, and a unique trace that is also the unique quasitracial state.
\end{introtheorem}

ref_dict.MainTh:
\begin{introcorollary}\label{MainTh}
    Let $G$ be a countable discrete group, and $\omega\in Z^2(G,\mathbb T)$. Assume that $G$ is either 
    \begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*,label=\textup{(\roman*)}]
        \item acylindrically hyperbolic, or
        \item a lattice in ${\rm SL}(n,\mathbb R)$, for $n\geq 2$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then the reduced twisted group \Cs-algebra $\Csr(G,\omega)$ is pure and has stable rank one. 
\end{introcorollary}

ref_dict.introB:
\begin{introtheorem}\label{introB}
    Let $G$ be a countable discrete group, $\widetilde{G}$ a finite-by-$G$ extension, and $\omega\in Z^2(\widetilde{G},\mathbb T)$. Assume that every  reduced twisted group \Cs-algebra over a finite index subgroup of $G$ is selfless with respect to the canonical trace.  Then $\Csr(\widetilde{G},\omega)$ is pure and has stable rank one.
\end{introtheorem}

